[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">The Jewels Of Babylon</a>
(credits, magazine scores and covertape).

[Fact:story:Queen Victoria's Wedding Present]
The jewels were made in Babylon some 3,000 years ago and reached English
hands by the end of the 19th century. Queen Victoria meant to give them to an
Indian princess as a wedding present, but pirates took the ship carrying them
off West Africa and left the crew for dead, all except you. See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:pink:A Pink Ship]
Computer &amp; Video Games complained that your ship is bright pink, "and no
amount of fiddling with the TV will make it go the colour you thought 19th
century ships were supposed to have been". See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:ladder:Climb Down Ladder]
Every reviewer struggled with the first move. "Down", "Go down" and
"Climb ladder" all fail; you need "CLIMB DOWN LADDER" and then "CLIMB INTO
BOAT". See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:stir:A Stir In The Interceptor Camp]
Crash's lukewarm review called the game "the same cake with a different
flavoured icing". In its later After Shock review Crash recalled that the
Jewels review had "caused a stir in the Interceptor camp". Sources:
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Jewels reviews</a>, <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">After Shock reviews</a>.

[Fact:scores:Reviews]
Scores: Big K 2/3, Computer &amp; Video Games 6/10, Crash issue 13 6/10,
Personal Computer Games 6/10, Personal Computer News 8/10, Sinclair User
issue 37 4/10. It was later given away on Sinclair User issue 88's Megatape
17 (July 1989). Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:crab:The Following Crab]
The giant crab can follow you from room to room. Bit 0 of #R$BC6F is set at
#R$EF54(#N$EF91) when you walk into room #N$30 while the crab is there, and
cleared at #R$EF54(#N$EF64) when you reach room #N($2C), or at #R$FB16(#N$FB43)
when the boulder falls on it. The other seven bits are spare, so the engine
could have had up to eight followers.

[Fact:snake:The Short-Sighted Water Snake]
The water snake at #R$EEFC leaves you alone most of the time. Only if a
one-in-four roll goes against you does it strike, and even then a second
roll at #R$EEFC(#N$EF0F), nominally one in #N($14), decides whether the bite
kills you. Both rolls come from the frame counter about #N$19 frames apart,
so in practice a strike kills you about three times in sixteen. Computer
&amp; Video Games said these encounters are "just for fun" most of the time.
See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6512"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:leftovers:Leftovers]
The dead-pirate table at #R$F8D9(#N$F904) has entries for the water snake
and the spider at #R$F8D9(#N$F907), which are never used because the search
at #R$F851(#N$F8C8) only ever finds pirates. Item #N$36 in
#R$FDB5(#N$FDEB) has no description and is never put into play, and the
scenic-event tables have spare slots at #R$BC81 and #R$BCDE.

[Fact:swearing:Don't Be Disgusting!]
Entries #R$E61D(#N$E81B) and #R$E61D(#N$E820) in the vocabulary are token
100 (#N$64), stored as "FUCK" and "SCRE" (the game only keeps the first four
letters of each word, so this is FUCK and SCREW). Swear at something and
#R$FB83 tells you off, and it remembers that you did. The first time you get
"Don't be disgusting!" (#R$E3A3), the second time "I've warned you before!
This deviant practice has got to stop." (#R$E3B8), and after that the game
just says "I didn't hear that!" (#R$E3F7) until you start a new game. Two
bits at #R$BC54 keep track of how many times you have sworn.
